            <ArticleTitle>A review of Agent Orange and its associated oncologic risk of genitourinary cancers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>633-639</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1078-1439(17)30463-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.urolonc.2017.08.029</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Agent Orange is an herbicide sprayed widely in Vietnam that is linked to a variety of malignancies in as early as 1991.Since then, there has been concern for, and subsequent interest in studying, the potential connection between Agent Orange and other malignancies. In the past 2 decades, there have been significant changes in the opinion of the National Academy of Science regarding Agent Orange and certain genitourinary malignancies. Herein, we review the literature regarding the potential link between Agent Orange and various urological cancers, including prostate, bladder, testicular, and renal cancers.</AbstractText>
